 is  based off of the  phenomenon of raman scattering which was  first  observed by stevie ramen who's pictured  here  in 1928 so in his experiment he isolated  a violet light and was able to pass it  through a liquid sample  so that liquid sample scattered the  radiation  and he observed two types of light that  was scattered one  that was also violet light and then one  that was scattered green light  and so if you look at our little  electromagnetic  spectrum at the bottom left we can see  that the green light  is at a lower energy than  the violet light and so we have two  types of scattering here  one that's at the same energy as our  incident light and one that's at a  different  energy and it's this uh  different energy scattering that we're  interested here  uh with raman spectroscopy  so this is just a drawing of the uh the  ramen effect  so we have our incident energy that  comes in  and excites the bonds from our molecule  and we see our two types of energy  that's uh scattered here so we see  our elastic scattering which is our  unchanged  scattering which is also called rayleigh  scattering so this is that the same  energy  as the energy of our excitation source  and then we also  have our ramen scattering that occurs so  that's our inelastic  scattering which is scattering that's  happening at a different energy  than the energy of our excitation source  so it's important to note here ramen  is an inherently insensitive analytical  technique so  for a lot of molecules it's difficult  for us  to reach really low limits of detection  with ramen and so it's for this reason  that we need a really strong and stable  light source  that can build up the amount of photons  that are  going into our sample so that we can  build up a strong signal  of raman scattered photons so typically  about one out of every  million incident photons are raman  scattered  so a raman spectrum is plotted with  intensity  on our y-axis and raman shift on our  x-axis so the raman 