good morning good afternoon and  welcome everyone to today's live  broadcast  biomedical applications of raman  spectroscopy  i'm alexis krause of labyrinths and i'll  be your moderator for today's event  today's educational web seminar is  brought to you by lab roots  and sponsored by metro usa for more  information about our sponsor  please visit their website at metrum.com  so let's get started i would like to  remind everyone that this event  is interactive we encourage you to  participate by submitting as many  questions as you want at any time you  want  during the presentation to do so simply  type them into the ask a question box  and click on the send button if you have  trouble seeing or hearing the  presentation  please click on the support tab found at  the top right of the presentation window  or report your problem by clicking on  the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  i'd like to now introduce our presenters  kristin frano  applications manager at bmw tech and dr  adam hopkins  spectroscopy product manager for  metromusa  for a complete biography on our  presenters please visit the biography  tab  at the top of your screen christian and  adam  you may now begin your presentations  welcome  thanks alexis um and thanks to those  who are joining in today um so we're  really excited to be  talking about this topic  which alexis like alexa said is  biomedical applications of raman  spectroscopy  so first i'll give a brief introduction  to some of the  basic principles of raman spectroscopy  and then we'll dive a little bit deeper  into discussing  some examples of biomedical applications  that our systems have been used for  recently  and then adam will give us an overview  of  the metro ramen spectroscopy portfolio  so when we talk about photography all  we're really talking about  here is the interaction of some kind of  light with some kind of matter  and in that interaction we learn  some information about our sample of  matter  and we get that in the form of an output  which  in spectroscopy is a graph that we call  a spectrum so you can see that little  picture of the raman spectrum  there so there's the output that we get  and then there's the actual information  that we can interpret  from the output that can help us solve  the problems that  we're trying to answer so from a raman  spectrum  we can get structural information about  our sample  that we can use to do either qualitative  or quantitative analysis  so qualitative analysis is based off of  the band positions  of the spectrum so raman spectroscopy is  a type of  vibrational spectroscopy um in which  we're probing  the vibrational groups  that are present in our sample so we're  looking at the actual chemical bonds  um in our sample so because  each uh each functional group has its  own  um kind of characteristic frequencies  at which it vibrates the raman spectrum  can act as a  molecular fingerprint that we can use to  identify a sample  so in this example you can see  the spectrum of the one butanol  and the four isobutyl acetyl phenone  that's the red spectrum and you can see  that because they're different  molecules they have distinct raman  spectra  that we can use to help identify them  and this is a big advantage because this  allows us to use spectral libraries  to identify either unknown samples or to  verify  the identity of known materials also  shifts in the peak positions in our  raman spectrum can give us  some information about changes that are  happening  in our sample matrix  for a quantitative analysis the  intensity of  our measured peaks that we see  is proportional to the amount of the  analyte that's present in our sample  matrix  so we can use the peak heights that we  see  to directly correlate the concentration  of the analyte that we have in our  sample so we can do  quantitative analysis based on peak  intensities  just a little bit of some historical  background  information so raman spectroscopy is  based off of the  phenomenon of raman scattering which was  first  observed by stevie ramen who's pictured  here  in 1928 so in his experiment he isolated  a violet light and was able to pass it  through a liquid sample  so that liquid sample scattered the  radiation  and he observed two types of light that  was scattered one  that was also violet light and then one  that was scattered green light  and so if you look at our little  electromagnetic  spectrum at the bottom left we can see  that the green light  is at a lower energy than  the violet light and so we have two  types of scattering here  one that's at the same energy as our  incident light and one that's at a  different  energy and it's this uh  different energy scattering that we're  interested here  uh with raman spectroscopy  so this is just a drawing of the uh the  ramen effect  so we have our incident energy that  comes in  and excites the bonds from our molecule  and we see our two types of energy  that's uh scattered here so we see  our elastic scattering which is our  unchanged  scattering which is also called rayleigh  scattering so this is that the same  energy  as the energy of our excitation source  and then we also  have our ramen scattering that occurs so  that's our inelastic  scattering which is scattering that's  happening at a different energy  than the energy of our excitation source  so it's important to note here ramen  is an inherently insensitive analytical  technique so  for a lot of molecules it's difficult  for us  to reach really low limits of detection  with ramen and so it's for this reason  that we need a really strong and stable  light source  that can build up the amount of photons  that are  going into our sample so that we can  build up a strong signal  of raman scattered photons so typically  about one out of every  million incident photons are raman  scattered  so a raman spectrum is plotted with  intensity  on our y-axis and raman shift on our  x-axis so the raman shift is calculated  by taking  the difference between the energy  of our incident light and the energy of  our scattered photons and so we measure  the raman shift in inverse centimeters  or wave numbers  and the the bands that are plotted  represent the vibrational modes of our  molecule so these correspond to  the different functional groups that  make up the molecule so  in this example we have a spectrum of  cyclohexane which is  our purple spectrum and the spectrum of  uh collected from benzene which is kind  of that bright green  spectrum here and looking at the two  structures of those molecules they may  seem  somewhat similar but due to the  differences in their chemical  bonding right we have our aromatic ring  for benzene so we have those  carbon-carbon double bonds  so based on the differences of their  actual chemical bonding  we see very distinct raman spectra  that we can use to do you know some sort  of  qualitative measurements off of so just  a couple of things  that are important to note about raman  spectra  so they are independent of the laser  excitation wavelength  that is used um and so for instance  to the right here we have a spectrum  or a set of spectra collected from  toluene  and so these sector are all collected  with three different  laser excitation wavelengths so 532  785 and 1064 nanometer lasers  but as you can see the peak positions  remain the same  throughout the set of spectra regardless  of what laser  is used so a question that we get asked  really  often is well okay  what laser should i use for my  experiment that's really going to  depend on the type of sample that you're  looking at  as well as some of the  kind of analytical needs of the  experiment as well  in regards to raman intensity  so the raman scattering strength is  proportional to  the energy of the laser that's used so  this means that the higher energy laser  we use the more intense our raman  spectrum  will be which also means that the longer  wavelength that we use  um the lower intensity our spectrum will  be because it's a lower energy  laser so because of that you might think  that we should always use  really high energy lasers so we can get  really good  raman signal all the time but one caveat  here  is that if we do use a  higher energy laser that it's possible  that that could cause  some fluorescence issues for us  um in our raman spectra so fluorescence  is a  competing technique um it's an emission  technique so the molecule  is excited and then emits  photons that are that are  fluorescence and fluorescence is a much  more efficient technique than raman is  and so if we excite the fluorescence in  our molecule  it's possible that that could overwhelm  the raman signal that we're trying  to observe um and then in that case we  won't get any peaks  that we're able to do either qualitative  or quantitative analysis with so  for instance the example that i have is  a ramen spectra collected from sesame  seed oil  using three different laser excitations  sesame seed oil is a really um a very  dark brown  oil and it's because of that that we see  some of the spectra specifically  the ones collected with 532 nanometer  laser  and a 785 nanometer laser in those cases  the spectra are  basically completely overwhelmed by the  fluorescence that's  generated um so we don't really see any  of the raman scattering that we're  interested in  and it's not until we shift using a  longer wavelength like a 1064 nanometer  wavelength  that we're kind of able to avoid that  fluorescence region  and then we're able to observe the raman  scattering from the sample that then we  can do analysis with  so over the past few decades there's  really been  a big push for ramen to enter  the biomedical fields um and the reason  for that is that there's a lot of  biomolecules um that are raman active  so these have functional groups that  have  um they have vibrations in their raman  spectra so we can use them  to do different types of studies  so tissue components like lipids  and proteins and fatty acids and  nucleic acids all have raman signal that  we can use  to study them and so the important  regions  for these materials come down to  kind of four main regions so one is  a our hydrogen stretching region from  about 2500 to  4000 wave numbers this may be important  to study  you know carbon hydrogen stretching in  proteins  triple bonds are found from 2000 to  2500 there's not a lot of really  important triple bonds in  biomedical analysis but that's where  they would  be present double bonds however are very  important  in things like lipids and those would be  found from 1500 to  2000 and then from 600 to 1500 we have  kind of our fingerprint region where we  really get the complex  patterns that we can use  as kind of an identifier for for our  specific molecule that we're looking at  so ramen has a lot of advantages for  biomedical analysis so for example ramen  is a non-destructive technique  and it's very highly selective compared  to  even other spectroscopic techniques like  near-infrared  spectroscopy where the bands you get  tend to be a lot more broader  water is also a very weak  ramen scatter so that's actually really  good because we get very little  interference  when we use water as our solvent so we  can measure aqueous solutions which is  really a big advantage in the  biomedical application also the  portability  and the fiber optics of today's raman  systems  really allow for flexible measurements  in kind of a lot of  non-laboratory settings so you can  actually see  to the right here some of the  examples of the fiber optic setups that  our portable raman has so  i think this is going to be really  important kind of going forward because  the trend  in biomedicine is going more and more  towards  portable analysis and really looking for  simple solutions that can be applied at  what's called the point of need  so this might be either a clinical  setting or an operating room  but this type this idea of portability  is going to be really important  moving forward and although ramen  is an inherently insensitive  technique we can actually use a  variation of  raman spectroscopy called surface  enhanced raman spectroscopy  or sirs and this can increase  the sensitivity of our signal by a  factor of about 10 to the third  to 10 to the seventh depending on  kind of what our experiment looks like  and this allows us to get down  to trace levels of  detection of biomaterials and so we'll  talk a little bit about  sirs in one of our examples  and i do just want to give a few  examples  of areas where our systems have  been used for biomedical analysis  recently so the first example that i'll  talk about  is the classification of cancer tissue  so cancer is the leading cause of death  worldwide  so you can understand you know why it's  been such a growing application  for raman spectroscopy in recent years  so because healthy tissue and cancer  tissue  have kind of their own different  biochemical compositions  we can actually use the differences in  their raman spectra  to distinguish healthy tissue from  cancer tissue  and so there's really a kind of a big  push for portable ramen  to be used in the space because like i  was saying then that means that your  measurements don't have to be done in a  lab they can be made  for example in an operating room and so  having  the portable raman system can kind of  provide a real-time guidance  for a surgeon  so this doesn't necessarily negate the  need for  pathology results but it does help  mitigate  the reliance on those results because  they may take  one to two weeks to come back  and meanwhile the patient is still kind  of in limbo  not necessarily knowing the concrete  results  of their their surgery so  in breast cancer lumpectomies for  example  the surgeon removes the cancerous tissue  and then what's called a margin analysis  is performed on the sample  so i'll share some research from harvey  mudd college  in collaboration with the city of hope  medical center  and the kenohi institute all in  california  where they used portable ramen to  sort of classify the cancer margins and  lumpectomy samples  so here's kind of a drawing which shows  the definitions of positive  and negative cancer margins so  if there is a cancer cells that are  present at the edge  of the tissue sample that would indicate  a positive margin test  so this indicates that it's likely that  there's still cancer tissue that's left  at the site of the  lumpectomy um on the other hand for a  negative test that means that there's no  cancer tissue  at the edge of the sample so it's a  likely indication  that the surgeon was able to remove it  all  and so in their experiment they measured  actually with two types of portable  raman systems  one with a 785 nanometer laser  and one with a 1064 nanometer laser  because they were worried about  fluorescence with the 785 nanometer  system  they also were able to sample using  kind of a variety of setups  as you can see in the pictures that are  shown using the portable  or using the fiber optics setup to  measure in kind of a  variety of ways the top one using a  probe  support stand also using a microscope  and then also using the handheld  fiber optic probe as well  they were able to acquire 50 tissue  samples  and while the pathologists had to  was classifying the samples as either  tissue samples  or sorry normal samples or cancerous  samples their raman spectra were  collected  at the same time  so here we have data sets collected from  two different  laser excitation wavelengths so the top  is the 1064 nanometer  data and the bottom is the 785 nanometer  data  so it's very difficult to detect the  differences here  if you don't know what you're looking  for so especially in the 1064 nanometer  data  because we have a little bit of  fluorescence in the 785 but for the 1064  nanometer data  the healthy tissue spectra looks quite