he bovine serum albumin at the  bottom  both fluoresce with 785 nanometer  excitation  so 1064 is really the best solution  for the cell culture media  so ramen can also be used in vaccine  characterization  and so this is work that marina  kirkatz's group  at sanofi pasteur in toronto  has done with their portable ramen  system so  they use ramen to characterize a new  tuberculosis  vaccine candidate so tuberculosis  is one of the top 10 causes of death  worldwide  and while there's a vaccine that exists  for it  its efficacy is variable especially in  adults so it's worth it to explore  new options for a tuberculosis vaccine  so for this vaccine candidate it's made  by adsorbing  an antigen protein h4  to the ic31 adjuvant  but during this process though they  think that there may be some structural  changes  to the h4 protein that occur upon  that absorption and these structural  changes are important for them  to understand so they want to study the  structure of the protein  while it's adsorbed to the adjuvant  because obviously if they absorb it and  then desorb it  that structure could then change again  so the h4 protein is made up of the  antigens  ag85b and tb  10.4 so you can see their structures  on the right and they're primarily made  up of  alpha helices and beta sheets  and so they used ramen to measure the  protein while it's adsorbed to the  adjuvant so that's the blue spectrum  on the left hand side and they also  measured  the adjuvant by itself which is the red  spectrum  and they measured just the free h4  protein by itself  and that's the green spectrum  so in the spectrum of the free h4  there's a very weak signal that's  consistent  with beta sheets present at 1670  wave numbers but when that h4  protein gets adsorbed to the adjuvant  which is the blue spectrum  we can see that that 1670 peak  significantly increases compared to the  spectrum  the green spectrum of the free h4  protein  so some of that signal can partially be  attributed to the adjuvant  so the red spectrum but not all of it  some of it can be attributed to  the conformational changes  just of the protein itself upon  absorption  so their results indicate that upon  adsorption to the edge event  the protein undergoes structural changes  that  involve kind of an increase in  the formation of these beta sheets  structurally  they did use some other analytical  techniques  that confirmed these findings but i  think this is a really good example  of using qualitative  changes in a raman data set  to kind of under help us understand the  processes that are happening  happening on a very fundamental level  and then i also wanted to touch briefly  on how ramen has been used for  disease detection specifically with  surface-enhanced raman scattering  so with disease detection the  trend here is to have really fast  simple and accurate measurements at the  point of need  so this could be in both first and third  world  countries um and so this is where  portable and handheld raman will really  be an advantage here  um and so for this example we're going  uh we're using tuberculosis  again which is a disease for which  diagnostic tasks are sometimes  inefficient  due to or insufficient due to  the the processing times associated with  the tests  the costs and the lack of portability  of these tests so again here it's  really worth it to explore new testing  options  so disease detection measurements really  need to have  a high sensitivity  and this is because the biomarkers that  we're usually interested  in to detect the disease are found in  very low concentrations  and really usually very complex matrices  like blood  or tissue and so it's for this reason  that we have to  go to surface enhanced raman  spectroscopy  to do these measurements um so  surface-enhanced ramen or sirs  is a variation of raman spectroscopy  but instead of our normal kind of  sampling setup that we have  on the lower left side of the screen  um instead we absorb our sample  to some sort of metal nano substrate  which is usually fabricated from either  gold or silver  so the enhancement that we see here  results  from the amplification of light  by the excitation of what's known as a  localized surface plasmon resonance or  lspr um and so our lspr  is just a kind of an oscillation  of the valence electrons that make up  our metals so you can kind of see  um that drawing of that on the right  there  and so this enhancement that we get can  be  again on the order from 10 to the third  to 10 to the seventh even greater  um depending on the sur's  there's active material that's used but  this enhancement really allows us to  reach  trace level detection of biomaterials  and so this is a research that's  presented in  collaboration with mark porter's group  from the university  of utah so to detect  tuberculosis biomarkers they came up  with  what's called a thurs sandwich styled  amino acid test  and this will detect and potentially  quantify  the amount of the tuberculosis marker  in human serum and this type of test is  based on  the binding of the antigen to  the antibodies um so  to prepare this test um they first had  to load the capture  antibody so it's the anti antigen  mab the red antibody on the upper  left part of the screen so they had to  load that onto the gold  glass and gold film support  using a coupling agent which is the dsp  and then the second part is they had to  make  what's called an extrinsic an extrinsic  ramen label  or an erl and so for this  they coupled gold nanoparticles  and that's what's going to give us our  sirs enhancement  so they couple the gold nanoparticles to  the  raman reporter molecule which in this  case is ds dsnb  um that's the little green uh the little  green picture that's shown on the lower  uh left-hand  corner um and then added the secondary  antibody to that  as well so the nanoparticle the raman  reporter and the secondary antibody all  make up that extrinsic  raman label  and so once the sample is loaded that  antigen  can be captured or sandwiched  between the capture antibody and the  secondary antibody  if it's present and then the third  measurement  is made and we're able to relate  either the presence of the raman  reporter molecule  um and the amount of it that's present  to either the presence and also the  amount that's present of the antigen  so the idea is that if there's no  antigen present  then the extrinsic raman label won't  bind to  won't be able to bind and so we won't  observe any of the signal from  the reporter label  so this allows for really selective  detection measurements of these type of  antigens  and they used a handheld raman system  specifically  to do these measurements um because this  allows them  kind of the portability that they need  to apply this measurement  at points of need outside of the  laboratory  so the detection levels for the antigen  are very low at nanogram per milliliter  level  and we can also potentially quantify  the amount of the antigen that's present  in a new sample from  a a patient  using this type of analysis so we can  create a calibration curve like the one  shown below here  okay so those are just a few examples of  some of the applications  that our systems have been used for  recently in the biomedical field  so now i will turn it over to adam who  will give us kind of an overview of  the metrum ramen spectroscopy portfolio  thanks kristen  i appreciate it and thank you for that  overview of  all the different uh of some of the  different types of applications and i  i know there are more out there that the  literature is filled with people  deploying raman spectroscopy and  innovative  approaches ultimately  when we talk about spectroscopy the  the reason that we talk about  spectroscopy and  part of the reason that it gets so much  is getting so much attention  is because of the the benefits that  spectroscopy can bring  less in the research and development  phase than in