 q a portion  of the webinar  if you have a question you'd like to ask  please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  and we'll answer as many of your  questions as we have time for  and as a reminder any questions that we  are unable to answer today  they will be addressed by the speakers  via the email address that you provided  at the time of registration so let's get  started with our first question  you mentioned ramen spec has no  interference from water  what about interference from biofluids  like blood  or urine  sure um so that's a good question um so  we don't have interference  from water um but other types  of uh solvents or biofluids like the  ones  mentioned um will have some raman signal  from them so  um that's why it's sort of dependent  experimentally if it's just  um the blood or the urine that we're  trying to  detect or compare to something else  then we can use that that signal but if  we're trying to measure  the very small amount of something  that's either in the blood  um or in the urine that's usually where  you know your surface enhanced raman  measurements where you have  a little bit more sample preparation  will come into play  perfect thank you christian for that  clarification so moving on to  question number two would this work for  metabolomics  for example analyzing media samples with  metabolites  produced by cells or bacteria  um yes so uh monitoring of  uh cell growth with ramen is a very  um sort of popular uh biopharmaceutical  process monitoring for uh for raman  spectroscopy  so measuring basically the  different metabolites that are produced  by  the cells as they're  consuming the media and so this is  usually done  building some sort of chemometric model  um for quantitative analysis  um where you have some known  concentrations to build your standards  off of  um and then with the  raman measurement you can do it um  just directly in the vessel so you don't  have to sam